# Governance manifest trust anchors (DEC-7301/DEC-7302).
#
# This file is the workflow-side half of the dual anchor: each line maps a
# governance manifest to the expected canonical SHA-256 of its parsed JSON
# (json.dumps with ensure_ascii=False, sort_keys=True, separators=(",",":")).
#
#   <manifest-path>:<64-hex canonical sha256>
#
# Consumed by .github/workflows/sps-ci.yml ("Governance manifest trust
# anchors" step) and cross-checked by
# framework/sps2/tests/test_manifest_anchor.py, which asserts this file's
# literals equal the module constants in legacy_exceptions.py /
# import_exceptions.py and the manifests' actual canonical hashes.
#
# WHY A SEPARATE FILE: sps-ci.yml is itself pinned in the legacy manifest.
# Embedding hash literals in the workflow would create a self-reference
# (workflow pins manifest hash; manifest pins workflow bytes) that never
# converges. This file breaks the cycle: it is NOT pinned in the manifest
# (that would re-create the loop one level up — manifest pins this file,
# this file holds the manifest's hash). Its protection is the anchor test
# (framework/sps2/tests/test_manifest_anchor.py asserts its literals equal
# the module constants and the manifests' actual canonical hashes) plus git
# history and PR review.
#
# Update procedure (order matters): edit the manifest(s) → re-pin via the
# standard re-pin flow → recompute canonical hashes → update the literals
# here AND the module constants → run test_manifest_anchor.py.
framework/sps2/governance/legacy-exception-manifest.json:863c6d62a3eea926888028587b628677bb6fd1d868fb5ecfbcd2c0448827847c
framework/sps2/governance/p10-p9-import-exception-manifest.json:ba0ce08bbad63224595b1bd1a1acedde66b30062be6680d25a5359bd745df819
